From e730ddf3a029887459918e898dcaeca99ee862fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 15:21:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] backends/color: Publish target primaries apart from the
 encoding

Like the luminance, the panel's native RGB primaries and white point
are declared in the DisplayID 2.0 Display Parameters data block, which
libdisplay-info exposes losslessly with
di_displayid2_data_block_get_native_chromaticities().

Keep the raw declarations next to the physical luminance, with explicit
unavailable, declared, invalid and conflicting states. Every Display
Parameters block is classified before deciding, so the result does not
depend on block order, and a block that cannot be interpreted is
reported as unsupported, separately from a recognized declaration with
unusable points (which keeps its raw counts).

On the native path, convert a complete CIE 1931 xy or CIE 1976 u'v'
declaration to protocol xy with exact integer arithmetic, and use it
only if the primaries form a non-degenerate triangle with the white
point strictly inside, both before and after quantization to protocol
units; otherwise the target primaries fall back to the complete
encoding primaries and white point.

Luminance and gamut stay independent. Both are updated together and
emit a single target-volume notification. The Wayland description
records carry the eight signed target coordinates, and target primaries
are sent separately from the encoding primaries. The PQ encoding,
reference white normalization and the renderer's color states are
unchanged.

meson.build now also probes for the chromaticity API.

Related: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/issues/5001
---
